## Part 1 — Quotes Database (same as before)

1. Upload `quotes-database.xlsx` to Google Drive; it converts to a
   Google Sheet automatically.
2. Row 2 on the **Quotes** and **Line Items** tabs is your real 734
   Rancho Cir quote. Add new quotes as new rows on both tabs, matched by
   Quote ID.
3. Fill in **Customer Phone** and **Customer Email** on each Quotes row
   so incoming messages can be matched to the right quote by sender.

## Part 2 — Install the chatbot backend

1. In that Sheet: **Extensions → Apps Script**. Delete any starter code,
   paste in `chatbot-quotes-and-messaging.gs`.
2. **Project Settings → Script Properties**, add `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`
   (from console.anthropic.com). You'll add four more properties below
   as you complete Parts 3 and 4.
3. **Deploy → New deployment → Web app**. Execute as **Me**, access
   **Anyone**. Deploy, authorize the permissions requested (Sheets +
   Gmail), and copy the **Web app URL** (ends in `/exec`).
4. Paste that URL into the `DEPLOYED_WEBAPP_URL` constant near the top
   of the script (needed for Part 3), then re-deploy (**Deploy → Manage
   deployments → Edit → New version**).

## Part 3 — SMS via SMS Gateway for Android (self-hosted, free)

This turns a spare Android phone with a SIM card into your SMS sender —
no monthly platform fee, you just pay for the phone's own cellular plan.
Project: **sms-gate.app** (open source, Apache-2.0 license).

1. On the Android phone: download the APK from
   github.com/capcom6/android-sms-gateway/releases and install it
   (enable "install from unknown sources" first).
2. Open the app, toggle **Cloud Server** on, tap **Online**. It shows a
   generated username and password — this is how the cloud relay
   authenticates requests, since the phone's own IP address can change.
3. In Apps Script **Script Properties**, add:
   - `SMSGATE_USERNAME` — from the app
   - `SMSGATE_PASSWORD` — from the app
4. In the Apps Script editor's function dropdown, select
   `registerSmsGatewayWebhook`, click **Run**, authorize if prompted.
   This tells the SMS Gateway cloud service to forward every incoming
   SMS to your Apps Script URL.
5. Text that phone's number from your own phone to test — a reply
   should arrive within a few seconds.

**Keep in mind:** the phone needs to stay powered on with a network
connection (Wi-Fi or data) to relay messages, and the project's own docs
note this isn't intended for high-volume batch sending — carriers may
throttle a consumer SIM sending a large volume of texts. Fine for a
typical small contractor's inbound-reply volume; not a bulk marketing
tool.

## Part 4 — WhatsApp via Meta's Cloud API (direct, no BSP)

This is Meta's own official API — no Twilio or other reseller in the
middle, so you pay Meta's base rate with no markup, and replies within
an open 24-hour conversation window are free.

1. Go to developers.facebook.com, create a Meta Developer account if you
   don't have one, then **My Apps → Create App → Business** type.
2. Add the **WhatsApp** product to the app. Meta gives you a free test
   phone number and a temporary access token automatically — good enough
   to test with immediately, no business verification required yet.
3. In **WhatsApp → API Setup**, note your **Phone Number ID**.
4. Generate a **permanent** access token: **Business Settings → Users →
   System Users → Add** → create a system user → assign your app with
   `whatsapp_business_messaging` and `whatsapp_business_management`
   permissions → generate token. (The default temporary token expires in
   24 hours — you want the permanent one.)
5. In Apps Script **Script Properties**, add:
   - `META_WHATSAPP_TOKEN` — the permanent token
   - `META_PHONE_NUMBER_ID` — from step 3
   - `META_VERIFY_TOKEN` — make up any string yourself, e.g.
     `usstoneworks-verify-2026`
6. Back in the Meta app dashboard: **WhatsApp → Configuration →
   Webhook**. Callback URL = your Apps Script `/exec` URL. Verify token
   = the same string you just made up. Meta will send a verification
   request; the script's `doGet` handles it automatically.
7. Subscribe the webhook to the **messages** field.
8. To actually receive real customer messages (not just your own test
   number), you'll eventually need **Business Verification** in Meta
   Business Manager — this can take anywhere from same-day to a couple
   of weeks depending on your documentation. Testing with the free test
   number works before that's done.

**Compliance note:** Meta's 2026 policy allows task-specific business
bots (support, sales, quotes — like this one) but disallows open-ended
"general purpose AI" bots on WhatsApp. This chatbot only ever answers
quote-lookup questions, so it fits the allowed category — just don't
repurpose it into an open-ended assistant on this channel.

## Part 5 — Email (unchanged)

1. In the Apps Script editor's function dropdown, select
   `enableEmailTrigger`, click **Run**, authorize Gmail access.
2. This checks the inbox every 5 minutes, replies in-thread to anything
   it can match to a quote, and labels handled emails
   `Chatbot-Replied` so nothing gets answered twice.

## Matching logic (all three channels, unchanged)

1. Message mentions a quote number (e.g. "388") → matched directly.
2. Otherwise, sender's phone number or email matches a Quotes row.
3. Otherwise, the message contains the property address.
4. No match → the bot asks for the address or quote number instead of
   guessing, and gives your phone number as a fallback.

## Reply footer

Every reply — whether a quote was found or not — now closes with a line
confirming email and text are both accepted, plus a link to the
website's Contact section:

> Email or text us anytime with questions — or visit
> https://us-stoneworks.github.io/us-stoneworks/#contact to reach us
> directly.

That URL and the fallback phone number live in two constants near the
top of the script — `CONTACT_URL` and `CONTACT_PHONE` — update them
there if the domain or number ever changes.

